  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Shower and thunderstorm chances later today, mainly \nfor the Southern Blue Ridge. Higher, and more widespread, shower and \nstorm chances for Thursday and Friday. Drier weather returns for the \nweekend.\n\nAs of early this morning, the surface high sits nearly overhead, \nallowing for calm winds and mostly clear skies, though some higher \nlevel clouds are moving in from the west, which may hamper some \nradiational cooling. Nevertheless, still expecting temperatures to \nfall into the 50s for most locations, perhaps even the 40s for some \nof the elevated valleys. Burkes Garden, VA is already sitting at\n48 degrees at this time.\n\nThrough the day, the high will shift offshore into the Atlantic, \nallowing for flow to become southerly, and advect more moisture into \nthe region. Temperatures will climb into the upper 70s in the \nmountains to mid to upper 80s in the Piedmont, about 5 or so degrees \nwarmer than yesterday. With some convergence via upslope flow along \nthe southern Blue Ridge, showers and storms are possible during the \nafternoon and evening hours, mostly across northwest NC and into \nsouthern VA. Any precipitation will wind down with the loss of \ndaytime heating after sunset. \n\nAn upper trough deepens over the eastern US by Thursday, with a \nsurface cold front progged to move through the Mid Atlantic Thursday \ninto Friday. There remains some uncertainty in the coverage and \ntiming of showers and storms associated with the frontal passage, as \nthe timing of the frontal passage itself may not quite coincide with \nthe strongest heating of the day and thus the greatest instability. \nTiming right now looks to be during the overnight hours of Thursday, \nas the front still lags across the Midwest Thursday afternoon. \nIf that scenario pans out, would more likely see scattered terrain\ndriven showers and storms Thursday given the moderately \nunstable airmass, and more widespread showers and storms on\nFriday, especially along and east of the Blue Ridge and over \nthe Carolinas, as the front traverses the area.\n\nAfter the front moves out of the area by the weekend, surface high \npressure builds back in from the west, bringing a return to drier \nweather by Sunday, though some lingering scattered showers and \nstorms are possible across NC Saturday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Heat builds back over the region by early next\nweek.\n\nSurface high pressure and 500mb ridging expanding across the \nsoutheastern states early next week will push temperatures back into \nthe 80s and 90s. Though subsidence from the upper ridge should \nlimit any precipitation chances, return flow around the high \nwill draw more moisture and warmer air from the south into the \nregion, thus bringing back the hot and muggy conditions typical \nof summertime.",
